<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/">Services</a></li><li>Appraisal Demand Representation</li></ol></nav><p class="tag">Appraisal service</p><h1>Appraisal Demand Representation for Ohio Policyholders</h1><p class="lede">When a written appraisal demand is on the table, the clock is running and the person you name matters. We act as your party appraiser from the first letter to the signed award.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-red" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-w" href="/contact-our-team/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Your role for us</dt><dd>Party appraiser</dd></div><div><dt>Common naming window</dt><dd>About 20 days</dd></div><div><dt>Award binds</dt><dd>Amount of loss</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>The moment appraisal is demanded</h2>
<p>An appraisal demand changes the tone of an Ohio claim. Before it, you are trading emails with an adjuster. After it, the policy’s appraisal clause sets the path: each side names a competent and impartial appraiser, the two appraisers try to agree on an umpire, and a written agreement by any two of the three fixes the amount of loss. Many Ohio forms give each side about 20 days to name its appraiser once the demand is received, so the first week is about reading the policy, not gathering opinions.</p>
<p>Appraisal demand representation in Ohio means we become that named appraiser for you. Whether you sent the demand or your carrier did, we confirm the deadlines in your specific form, respond in writing and start building a number that can stand on its own in front of an umpire.</p>
<h2>What we take on once we are named</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Policy and correspondence review.</strong> We read the declarations, endorsements and every letter on the claim so we know what has been paid, what was denied and what is truly in dispute.</li>
<li><strong>Independent inspection.</strong> We walk the property ourselves — attic, basement, roof slopes, siding elevations — instead of marking up the carrier’s estimate.</li>
<li><strong>A line-item valuation</strong> priced for your part of Ohio, with replacement cost, depreciation and any code items separated the way your policy treats them.</li>
<li><strong>Working sessions with the carrier’s appraiser</strong> to narrow the gap item by item. Many disputes settle here without an umpire ever ruling.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire selection and presentation</strong> if items are still open, with photos, measurements and pricing support for each one.</li>
<li><strong>Award review</strong> so the final form clearly states replacement cost, actual cash value and any recoverable holdback.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Coverage is a separate question</b>Appraisal sets how much the covered loss costs. It commonly does not decide whether a loss is covered at all, and an appraiser cannot give legal advice. If your claim was denied outright, or the suit-limitation date in your policy is close, talk to an Ohio attorney before relying on appraisal alone.</div>
<h2>Impartial does not mean passive</h2>
<p>Many Ohio policies require each appraiser to be “competent and impartial” or “independent.” That limits who can serve — your roofer, for example, usually should not — but it does not stop your appraiser from doing the work thoroughly. We build our valuation from the evidence, explain it plainly and defend it with documentation. An inflated number tends to lose credibility with an umpire; a well-supported one tends to move the award.</p>
<h2>Timing traps worth checking now</h2>
<p>Two dates matter on most Ohio claims. The first is the appraiser-naming window that starts when a demand is received. The second is the policy’s suit-limitation clause — often one year from the date of loss on Ohio homeowner forms, sometimes two — which Ohio courts generally enforce. Appraisal does not always pause that clock, so check your policy and ask counsel how it applies to your claim. Our <a href="/appraisal-workbench/claim-calendar-builder/">claim calendar builder</a> can lay out the dates side by side, and the <a href="/appraisal-timeline/">appraisal timeline guide</a> walks through each stage.</p>
<h2>Who this service suits</h2>
<p>Representation fits best when coverage is accepted and the disagreement is about scope or price: how many slopes need replacing on a Canton colonial, whether plaster walls in a Lakewood double can be patched, what a Dayton basement finish really costs. If you are not sure appraisal is the right move yet, start with an <a href="/appraisal-practice/estimate-audit/">estimate audit</a> to see where the gap actually sits.</p>
<h2>Questions about appraisal demand representation</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My insurer sent me an appraisal demand — what do I do first?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Read the appraisal clause in your policy and note any deadline for naming your appraiser, which on many Ohio forms is about 20 days. Then choose an appraiser who meets the policy’s impartiality language and confirm the choice in writing. If you have coverage questions or a suit deadline approaching, talk to an Ohio attorney as well.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I demand appraisal myself, or does the insurer have to start it?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Most Ohio appraisal clauses let either party make the written demand once there is a disagreement on the amount of loss. Check your policy for any steps it requires first, such as submitting a proof of loss. The demand should be in writing and kept with your claim file.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays the umpire in an Ohio appraisal?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Under most policy wording, each side pays its own appraiser and the two sides split the umpire’s fee and other shared appraisal costs. Your policy is the controlling document, so confirm the exact split there.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does a signed appraisal award end my claim?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>An award signed by two of the three panel members commonly sets the amount of loss, and the insurer then pays it subject to the deductible, prior payments and policy terms. Coverage disputes and other legal issues may still be open, which is a question for an Ohio attorney.</p></div></details></div>
